{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NiNAPazQaBGPjxfSnkJ6VL","type":"tool_result","content":"# Eviction Thresholds\n\nThe kubelet supports both soft and hard eviction thresholds.\n\n### Soft eviction thresholds\n\nA soft eviction threshold pairs an eviction signal with a grace period. The kubelet does not evict pods until the node conditions have been violated for the entire grace period. If no grace period is set, the kubelet evicts pods immediately upon reaching the threshold.\n\nYou can configure soft eviction thresholds using the following kubelet flags:\n\n- `--eviction-soft`: A set of eviction thresholds (e.g. `memory.available<1.5Gi`) that if met over a corresponding grace period would trigger a pod eviction.\n- `--eviction-soft-grace-period`: A set of eviction grace periods (e.g. `memory.available=1m30s`) that correspond to how long a soft eviction threshold must be met before a pod is evicted.\n- `--eviction-max-pod-grace-period`: The maximum allowed grace period (in seconds) of the dying pod before it is killed with a kill signal.\n\n### Hard eviction thresholds\n\nA hard eviction threshold has no grace period, and if observed, the kubelet will immediately evict pods.\n\nYou can configure hard eviction thresholds using the following kubelet flag:\n\n- `--eviction-hard`: A set of eviction thresholds (e.g. `memory.available<100Mi`) that if met would trigger immediate pod eviction.\n\nThe kubelet has the following default hard eviction thresholds:\n\n- `memory.available<100Mi`\n- `nodefs.available<10%`\n- `nodefs.available<5Gi`\n- `imagefs.available<15%`\n- `imagefs.available<5Gi`\n- `containerfs.available<15%`\n- `containerfs.available<5Gi`\n- `nodefs.inodesFree<4%`\n- `imagefs.inodesFree<4%`\n- `containerfs.inodesFree<4%`\n- `pid.available<0.5%`\n\nEviction monitoring interval[](#eviction-monitoring-interval)\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe kubelet evaluates eviction thresholds at a frequency specified by the `--housekeeping-interval` flag (default `10s`).\n\nNode conditions[](#node-conditions)\n-----------------------------------\n\nThe kubelet maps one or more eviction signals to a Kubelet Node Condition. If a hard eviction threshold has been met, or a soft eviction threshold has been met for the specified grace period, the kubelet reports a node condition (`True`) to reflect that the node is under pressure.\n\nThe following node conditions are supported by the kubelet:\n\nNode Condition\n\nEviction Signal\n\nDescription\n\n`MemoryPressure`\n\n`memory.available`\n\nAvailable memory on the node has satisfied an eviction threshold\n\n`DiskPressure`\n\n`nodefs.available`, `nodefs.inodesFree`, `imagefs.available`, `imagefs.inodesFree`, `containerfs.available`, `containerfs.inodesFree`\n\nAvailable disk space and inodes on node has satisfied an eviction threshold\n\n`PIDPressure`\n\n`pid.available`\n\nAvailable processes on the node has satisfied an eviction threshold\n\n### Node condition oscillation[](#node-condition-oscillation)\n\nIn some cases, nodes oscillate above and below a soft eviction threshold with the grace period not being met, causing the corresponding node condition to oscillate between `true` and `false`, and triggering eviction of pods. To prevent this oscillation, you can use the `--eviction-pressure-transition-period` flag to specify how long the kubelet must wait before transitioning a node condition to `false`. The default is `5m`.\n\n### Reclaiming node level resources[](#reclaim-node-resources)\n\nIf an eviction signal has not reached a threshold, but the kubelet observes that a node-level resource is starved, the kubelet attempts to reclaim that resource before evicting end-user pods. For example, when disk space is running low, the kubelet reclaims disk space by clearing out unused images or deleting dead containers and their logs.\n\nThe kubelet performs the following operations, depending on the eviction signal:\n\n`imagefs.available` or `imagefs.inodesFree`\n\nThe kubelet deletes unused images.\n\n`nodefs.available` or `nodefs.inodesFree`\n\nThe kubelet deletes unused images and dead containers and their logs.\n\n`containerfs.available` or `containerfs.inodesFree`\n\nThe kubelet deletes dead containers and their logs.\n\n### Pod selection for kubelet eviction[](#pod-selection-for-kubelet-eviction)\n\nIf reclaiming node-level resources is insufficient, the kubelet evicts pods. The kubelet ranks pods for eviction by using the following criteria, in order of precedence:\n\n1. Whether pod resource usage exceeds its requests\n2. Pod [priority](/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/pod-priority-preemption/#pod-priority)\n3. Pod resource usage relative to requests\n\nWhenever the kubelet needs to evict pods, it ranks the Pods as follows:\n\n1. If a pod's resource usage does not exceed its requests, the pod is ranked lower for eviction than other pods whose usage exceeds their requests. Pods that do not exceed their requests are ranked by their [QoS class](/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-qos/):\n   - `BestEffort` Pods are ranked highest for eviction\n   - `Burstable` Pods have a medium eviction priority\n   - `Guaranteed` Pods are ranked lowest for eviction\n\n2. If two pods have the same QoS class, the pod with the highest resource usage is ranked highest for eviction.\n\n3. If a pod's resource usage exceeds its requests, it is ranked higher for eviction than other pods. Among these pods:\n   - `Guaranteed` pods are ranked highest for eviction\n   - `Burstable` pods have a medium eviction priority\n   - `BestEffort` pods are ranked lowest for eviction\n   - Among pods of the same QoS class, the pod with the highest resource usage is ranked highest for eviction.\n\n#### kubelet eviction: pod selection based on QoS class\n\n| QoS Class | Requests | Usage | Rank |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `BestEffort` | not set | above requests | lowest |\n| `Burstable` | set | above requests | medium |\n| `Guaranteed` | set | above requests | highest |\n| `Guaranteed` | set | below requests | lowest |\n| `Burstable` | set | below requests | medium |\n| `BestEffort` | not set | below requests | highest |\n\nNode out of memory behavior[](#node-out-of-memory-behavior)\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nIf the node experiences an out-of-memory (OOM) condition before the kubelet can reclaim memory, the node's OOM killer takes action. The OOM killer selects and kills processes based on an `oom_score_adj` value set by the kubelet for each container.\n\nThe kubelet sets the `oom_score_adj` value for each container based on the pod's QoS class:\n\nQoS Class\n\noom_score_adj\n\n`Guaranteed`\n\n\\-997\n\n`BestEffort`\n\n1000\n\n`Burstable`\n\nmin(max(2, 1000 - (1000 * memoryRequestBytes) / memoryLimitBytes), 999)\n\nIf the node becomes out of memory before eviction occurs, the OOM killer will attempt to evict containers based on their `oom_score_adj` values. Containers with lower `oom_score_adj` values are considered less likely candidates for eviction. `Guaranteed` pods, which have low `oom_score_adj` values, are less likely to be selected by the OOM killer. `BestEffort` pods, which have `oom_score_adj` values of 1000, are the most likely to be selected. For `Burstable` pods, the `oom_score_adj` value is between 2 and 999.\n\nGood practices[](#node-pressure-eviction-good-practices)\n--------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Schedulable resources and eviction policies\n\nWhen you configure the kubelet's eviction policies, make sure that you also configure the [node allocatable](/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/#node-allocatable) settings properly. The kubelet uses these settings when scheduling pods and making eviction decisions.\n\nGuidelines:\n\n- Eviction thresholds should be set lower than the [node allocatable](/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/#node-allocatable) settings to provide a buffer between the eviction threshold and the amount of resources reserved for system daemons.\n- The amount of memory set aside as a buffer should be enough to absorb temporary spikes in memory demand.\n- Hard eviction thresholds should not be set aggressively, as this could result in poor scheduling decisions and premature pod evictions.\n\n### DaemonSets and node-pressure eviction\n\nThe kubelet does not evict pods created by a DaemonSet controller. This is because DaemonSets are intended to run on every node in the cluster and are critical to cluster health.\n\nIf the kubelet were to evict pods created by DaemonSets, the controller would immediately recreate the pods, potentially causing a cycle where the DaemonSet pods are constantly evicted and recreated. This could disrupt cluster operations.\n\nHowever, for other workload types (Deployments, StatefulSets, etc.), the kubelet will evict pods as needed to reclaim resources, and the workload controller will recreate the pods on other nodes if available.\n\nKnown issues[](#known-issues)\n-----------------------------\n\n### kubelet may not observe memory pressure right away[](#kubelet-may-not-observe-memory-pressure-right-away)\n\nThe kubelet calculates `memory.available` based on cgroup data. On some systems, the cgroup memory stats can exhibit significant delays (up to several seconds) in reflecting actual memory pressure on the node. This delay can cause the kubelet to not observe memory pressure right away, which can result in delayed eviction decisions.\n\n### active_file memory is not considered as available memory[](#active-file-memory-is-not-considered-as-available-memory)\n\nOn Linux, the kubelet calculates `memory.available` by excluding `inactive_file` from the calculation. However, in some cases, active file-backed memory (the `active_file` cgroup stat) is not readily reclaimable. For example, page cache for files that are being actively read or written is tracked as `active_file`, and the kernel may not reclaim this memory immediately. In such cases, the kubelet's eviction decisions may not align with the node's actual ability to free memory.\n\nWhat's next[](#what-s-next)\n---------------------------\n\n- See [Node allocatable](/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/#node-allocatable)\n- See [API-initiated eviction](/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/api-eviction/)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e4cae61e-42df-484d-af84-1dcbf8c0a281","uuid":"058befb2-346a-4c14-98c0-2b926834dfab","timestamp":"2026-08-31T06:45:07.406Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":534504,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Eviction Thresholds\n\nThe kubelet supports both soft and hard eviction thresholds.\n\n### Soft eviction thresholds\n\nA soft eviction threshold pairs an eviction signal with a grace period. The kubelet does not evict pods until the node conditions have been violated for the entire grace period. If no grace period is set, the kubelet evicts pods immediately upon reaching the threshold.\n\nYou can configure soft eviction thresholds using the following kubelet flags:\n\n- `--eviction-soft`: A set of eviction thresholds (e.g. `memory.available<1.5Gi`) that if met over a corresponding grace period would trigger a pod eviction.\n- `--eviction-soft-grace-period`: A set of eviction grace periods (e.g. `memory.available=1m30s`) that correspond to how long a soft eviction threshold must be met before a pod is evicted.\n- `--eviction-max-pod-grace-period`: The maximum allowed grace period (in seconds) of the dying pod before it is killed with a kill signal.\n\n### Hard eviction thresholds\n\nA hard eviction threshold has no grace period, and if observed, the kubelet will immediately evict pods.\n\nYou can configure hard eviction thresholds using the following kubelet flag:\n\n- `--eviction-hard`: A set of eviction thresholds (e.g. `memory.available<100Mi`) that if met would trigger immediate pod eviction.\n\nThe kubelet has the following default hard eviction thresholds:\n\n- `memory.available<100Mi`\n- `nodefs.available<10%`\n- `nodefs.available<5Gi`\n- `imagefs.available<15%`\n- `imagefs.available<5Gi`\n- `containerfs.available<15%`\n- `containerfs.available<5Gi`\n- `nodefs.inodesFree<4%`\n- `imagefs.inodesFree<4%`\n- `containerfs.inodesFree<4%`\n- `pid.available<0.5%`\n\nEviction monitoring interval[](#eviction-monitoring-interval)\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe kubelet evaluates eviction thresholds at a frequency specified by the `--housekeeping-interval` flag (default `10s`).\n\nNode conditions[](#node-conditions)\n-----------------------------------\n\nThe kubelet maps one or more eviction signals to a Kubelet Node Condition. If a hard eviction threshold has been met, or a soft eviction threshold has been met for the specified grace period, the kubelet reports a node condition (`True`) to reflect that the node is under pressure.\n\nThe following node conditions are supported by the kubelet:\n\nNode Condition\n\nEviction Signal\n\nDescription\n\n`MemoryPressure`\n\n`memory.available`\n\nAvailable memory on the node has satisfied an eviction threshold\n\n`DiskPressure`\n\n`nodefs.available`, `nodefs.inodesFree`, `imagefs.available`, `imagefs.inodesFree`, `containerfs.available`, `containerfs.inodesFree`\n\nAvailable disk space and inodes on node has satisfied an eviction threshold\n\n`PIDPressure`\n\n`pid.available`\n\nAvailable processes on the node has satisfied an eviction threshold\n\n### Node condition oscillation[](#node-condition-oscillation)\n\nIn some cases, nodes oscillate above and below a soft eviction threshold with the grace period not being met, causing the corresponding node condition to oscillate between `true` and `false`, and triggering eviction of pods. To prevent this oscillation, you can use the `--eviction-pressure-transition-period` flag to specify how long the kubelet must wait before transitioning a node condition to `false`. The default is `5m`.\n\n### Reclaiming node level resources[](#reclaim-node-resources)\n\nIf an eviction signal has not reached a threshold, but the kubelet observes that a node-level resource is starved, the kubelet attempts to reclaim that resource before evicting end-user pods. For example, when disk space is running low, the kubelet reclaims disk space by clearing out unused images or deleting dead containers and their logs.\n\nThe kubelet performs the following operations, depending on the eviction signal:\n\n`imagefs.available` or `imagefs.inodesFree`\n\nThe kubelet deletes unused images.\n\n`nodefs.available` or `nodefs.inodesFree`\n\nThe kubelet deletes unused images and dead containers and their logs.\n\n`containerfs.available` or `containerfs.inodesFree`\n\nThe kubelet deletes dead containers and their logs.\n\n### Pod selection for kubelet eviction[](#pod-selection-for-kubelet-eviction)\n\nIf reclaiming node-level resources is insufficient, the kubelet evicts pods. The kubelet ranks pods for eviction by using the following criteria, in order of precedence:\n\n1. Whether pod resource usage exceeds its requests\n2. Pod [priority](/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/pod-priority-preemption/#pod-priority)\n3. Pod resource usage relative to requests\n\nWhenever the kubelet needs to evict pods, it ranks the Pods as follows:\n\n1. If a pod's resource usage does not exceed its requests, the pod is ranked lower for eviction than other pods whose usage exceeds their requests. Pods that do not exceed their requests are ranked by their [QoS class](/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-qos/):\n   - `BestEffort` Pods are ranked highest for eviction\n   - `Burstable` Pods have a medium eviction priority\n   - `Guaranteed` Pods are ranked lowest for eviction\n\n2. If two pods have the same QoS class, the pod with the highest resource usage is ranked highest for eviction.\n\n3. If a pod's resource usage exceeds its requests, it is ranked higher for eviction than other pods. Among these pods:\n   - `Guaranteed` pods are ranked highest for eviction\n   - `Burstable` pods have a medium eviction priority\n   - `BestEffort` pods are ranked lowest for eviction\n   - Among pods of the same QoS class, the pod with the highest resource usage is ranked highest for eviction.\n\n#### kubelet eviction: pod selection based on QoS class\n\n| QoS Class | Requests | Usage | Rank |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `BestEffort` | not set | above requests | lowest |\n| `Burstable` | set | above requests | medium |\n| `Guaranteed` | set | above requests | highest |\n| `Guaranteed` | set | below requests | lowest |\n| `Burstable` | set | below requests | medium |\n| `BestEffort` | not set | below requests | highest |\n\nNode out of memory behavior[](#node-out-of-memory-behavior)\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nIf the node experiences an out-of-memory (OOM) condition before the kubelet can reclaim memory, the node's OOM killer takes action. The OOM killer selects and kills processes based on an `oom_score_adj` value set by the kubelet for each container.\n\nThe kubelet sets the `oom_score_adj` value for each container based on the pod's QoS class:\n\nQoS Class\n\noom_score_adj\n\n`Guaranteed`\n\n\\-997\n\n`BestEffort`\n\n1000\n\n`Burstable`\n\nmin(max(2, 1000 - (1000 * memoryRequestBytes) / memoryLimitBytes), 999)\n\nIf the node becomes out of memory before eviction occurs, the OOM killer will attempt to evict containers based on their `oom_score_adj` values. Containers with lower `oom_score_adj` values are considered less likely candidates for eviction. `Guaranteed` pods, which have low `oom_score_adj` values, are less likely to be selected by the OOM killer. `BestEffort` pods, which have `oom_score_adj` values of 1000, are the most likely to be selected. For `Burstable` pods, the `oom_score_adj` value is between 2 and 999.\n\nGood practices[](#node-pressure-eviction-good-practices)\n--------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Schedulable resources and eviction policies\n\nWhen you configure the kubelet's eviction policies, make sure that you also configure the [node allocatable](/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/#node-allocatable) settings properly. The kubelet uses these settings when scheduling pods and making eviction decisions.\n\nGuidelines:\n\n- Eviction thresholds should be set lower than the [node allocatable](/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/#node-allocatable) settings to provide a buffer between the eviction threshold and the amount of resources reserved for system daemons.\n- The amount of memory set aside as a buffer should be enough to absorb temporary spikes in memory demand.\n- Hard eviction thresholds should not be set aggressively, as this could result in poor scheduling decisions and premature pod evictions.\n\n### DaemonSets and node-pressure eviction\n\nThe kubelet does not evict pods created by a DaemonSet controller. This is because DaemonSets are intended to run on every node in the cluster and are critical to cluster health.\n\nIf the kubelet were to evict pods created by DaemonSets, the controller would immediately recreate the pods, potentially causing a cycle where the DaemonSet pods are constantly evicted and recreated. This could disrupt cluster operations.\n\nHowever, for other workload types (Deployments, StatefulSets, etc.), the kubelet will evict pods as needed to reclaim resources, and the workload controller will recreate the pods on other nodes if available.\n\nKnown issues[](#known-issues)\n-----------------------------\n\n### kubelet may not observe memory pressure right away[](#kubelet-may-not-observe-memory-pressure-right-away)\n\nThe kubelet calculates `memory.available` based on cgroup data. On some systems, the cgroup memory stats can exhibit significant delays (up to several seconds) in reflecting actual memory pressure on the node. This delay can cause the kubelet to not observe memory pressure right away, which can result in delayed eviction decisions.\n\n### active_file memory is not considered as available memory[](#active-file-memory-is-not-considered-as-available-memory)\n\nOn Linux, the kubelet calculates `memory.available` by excluding `inactive_file` from the calculation. However, in some cases, active file-backed memory (the `active_file` cgroup stat) is not readily reclaimable. For example, page cache for files that are being actively read or written is tracked as `active_file`, and the kernel may not reclaim this memory immediately. In such cases, the kubelet's eviction decisions may not align with the node's actual ability to free memory.\n\nWhat's next[](#what-s-next)\n---------------------------\n\n- See [Node allocatable](/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/#node-allocatable)\n- See [API-initiated eviction](/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/api-eviction/)","durationMs":27951,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/node-pressure-eviction/"}}
